## Computational Structural Analysis — q=4

The requested structural data mining on the **already-generated 138-record dataset** is complete.

No all-tree enumeration was performed, and the burning-number algorithm was not modified.

### Core results

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

There are:

$$
\boxed{138}
$$

partition patterns for \(S=0,\ldots,10\), comprising

$$
2\text{ with }b(T)=2,\qquad
58\text{ with }b(T)=3,\qquad
78\text{ with }b(T)=4.
$$

The validation stored in the source JSON passed:

* \(S=0\) is exactly \(K_{1,9}\);
* \(b(K_{1,9})=2\);
* the complete \(S=6\) slice reproduces the previous 11-pattern experiment;
* \((2,2,2,0,\ldots,0)\) is the unique \(S=6\) pattern with \(b(T)=4\).

### One-variable threshold search

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

None of the requested 22 statistics gives a perfect separator of \(b=3\) and \(b=4\) using a rule

$$
F(s)\ge C.
$$

None gives a perfect separator using \(F(s)\le C\) either.

The strongest individual thresholds among the tested statistics are:

| Statistic      |          Best threshold |       Errors |
| -------------- | ----------------------: | -----------: |
| top-3 sum      |                \(\ge7\) | **17 / 136** |
| excess above 1 |                \(\ge5\) | **17 / 136** |
| sum of squares |               \(\ge19\) |           26 |
| sum of cubes   | \(\ge40,41,\) or \(43\) |           29 |
| max part       |                \(\ge3\) |           43 |

The complete counterexample lists for every failed threshold rule are in the report and JSON.

### Partition length

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

The \(b=4\) region first appears at \(S=6\).

The numbers of \(b=4\) patterns by \(S\) are:

$$
\boxed{
1,7,14,22,34
}
$$

for \(S=6,7,8,9,10\), respectively.

By number \(k\) of positive parts, \(b=4\) occurs for

$$
k=1,2,3,4,5,6,7,
$$

so \(k\) alone does not separate the classes.

### Majorization

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

There are

$$
\boxed{1540}
$$

comparable same-\(S\) partition pairs.

Both possible monotonicity directions fail.

For

$$
a\succeq b,
$$

the proposed implication

$$
b(T_a)\ge b(T_b)
$$

has

$$
\boxed{44}
$$

counterexamples.

The opposite implication

$$
b(T_a)\le b(T_b)
$$

has

$$
\boxed{482}
$$

counterexamples.

A particularly simple \(S=6\) counterexample to the first direction is

$$
(6,0,\ldots,0)\succeq(2,2,2,0,\ldots,0),
$$

but

$$
3<4.
$$

The converse direction also fails because

$$
(2,2,2,0,\ldots,0)
$$

dominates both

$$
(2,2,1,1,0,\ldots,0)
$$

and

$$
(2,1,1,1,1,0,\ldots,0),
$$

while their burning number drops from \(4\) to \(3\).

The **complete 526-counterexample list** is included in the report.

### Two-variable rules

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

None of the seven requested pairs admits a perfect axis-aligned AND/OR threshold rule.

Best errors:

| Pair                    | Best errors |
| ----------------------- | ----------: |
| \((m_1,k)\)             |          36 |
| \((\#\ge2,\#\ge3)\)     |          25 |
| \((\#=2,\#=3)\)         |          53 |
| \((m_1,\#\ge2)\)        |      **18** |
| \((\sum s_i^2,k)\)      |          24 |
| \((m_1,m_2)\)           |          20 |
| \((\text{top3 sum},k)\) |          17 |

The nominal 17-error rule for `(top3 sum, k)` is actually degenerate because the best rule does not use \(k\) substantively; the best genuinely two-coordinate rule among the tested pairs has 18 errors:

$$
m_1\ge5
\quad\text{OR}\quad
\#\{i:s_i\ge2\}\ge3.
$$

It is still not exact.

### Strongest finite-data description

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

The strongest exact statement supported by this dataset is:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
S\le5 \implies b(T)\ne4,\\[2mm]
S=6\text{ has exactly one }b=4\text{ partition},\\[2mm]
S=7,8,9,10\text{ contain increasingly many }b=4\text{ partitions},\\[2mm]
\text{no tested scalar threshold, specified 2D threshold rule,}\\
\text{or either majorization monotonicity direction exactly classifies }b(T).
\end{array}}
$$

Thus the finite \(b=4\) region is best represented by its **explicit partition set**, rather than by a single concentration statistic.

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The data suggest that the **full partition shape** matters substantially: neither concentration, maximum arm subdivision, number of subdivided edges, nor the requested simple combinations captures the transition exactly.

That is only a hypothesis about this finite \(K_{1,9}\), \(S\le10\) dataset.

### Direct answers

**Q1. Is there a single simple statistic that separates \(b=3\) and \(b=4\)?**

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA] **No.**

**Q2. Is burning number monotone under partition majorization?**

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA] **No in either direction.**

**Q3. Is there a simple two-variable structural rule?**

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA] **No among the seven specified pairs and tested axis-aligned threshold forms.**

**Q4. What is the strongest empirically supported description of the \(b=4\) region?**

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA] It begins at \(S=6\), expands rapidly for \(S=7,\ldots,10\), and cannot be exactly characterized by any of the tested one- or two-statistic threshold rules.

**Q5. What is the most mathematically promising next experiment?**

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS] Continue the same \(K_{1,9}\) partition scan to larger \(S\), retaining the full partition shape and testing where \(b(T)=5\) first appears. This would directly probe whether the finite \(S\le10\) behavior is part of a larger pattern.

